WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.725
This is how Auracle makes sound, from
the patch graph to the live voices.

00:00:07.343 --> 00:00:11.170
Underneath is quiver, a modular
synthesis library in Rust.

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:14.598
On each tick, one sample
moves through the whole graph.

00:00:14.998 --> 00:00:20.642
Continuous knobs are atomic values the
audio thread reads, so turning one needs no

00:00:20.642 --> 00:00:21.642
recompile.

00:00:23.057 --> 00:00:27.725
The palette has forty-two modules, from
a plucked string to sidechained dynamics.

00:00:28.125 --> 00:00:33.681
The filter is a state variable design, or
a diode ladder that saturates harder one

00:00:33.681 --> 00:00:34.681
way.

00:00:34.352 --> 00:00:39.566
Audio and modulation are separate Rust
types, so a mistyped patch cannot even be

00:00:39.566 --> 00:00:40.566
built.

00:00:41.629 --> 00:00:46.814
Every voice ends with a DC blocker where
needed, an exponential envelope, and a

00:00:46.814 --> 00:00:47.814
limiter.

00:00:47.739 --> 00:00:52.996
Resonance and feedback are capped, so filters
cannot oscillate and delays cannot run

00:00:52.996 --> 00:00:53.996
away.

00:00:54.486 --> 00:00:58.408
For comparison, every patch plays
the same five second phrase.

00:00:58.808 --> 00:01:02.771
It holds a C, stabs an octave
higher, and plays a two note chord.

00:01:03.171 --> 00:01:05.811
It ends on a low C, with a long release.

00:01:06.211 --> 00:01:10.893
The random seed is reset every render,
so the samples repeat bit for bit.

00:01:12.343 --> 00:01:14.843
First, each raw render
goes through a gate.

00:01:15.243 --> 00:01:19.738
It fails silence, runaway peaks,
and signals dominated by DC.

00:01:20.138 --> 00:01:21.753
What fails is never played.

00:01:23.057 --> 00:01:27.690
Loudness is measured the broadcast
way, with K weighting and gated blocks.

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:32.595
Each patch is matched to minus eighteen
loudness units, since louder wins

00:01:32.595 --> 00:01:33.595
comparisons.

00:01:33.735 --> 00:01:38.152
The gain stops short of clipping instead
of limiting, so timbre is untouched.

00:01:39.486 --> 00:01:42.173
From that render come
eighteen audio features.

00:01:42.573 --> 00:01:47.713
Four measure the spectrum's brightness and
its movement, on a logarithmic frequency

00:01:47.713 --> 00:01:48.713
axis.

00:01:48.423 --> 00:01:52.816
Texture, level and envelope take
seven more, and one measures the bass.

00:01:53.216 --> 00:01:55.174
Three are read from single notes.

00:01:55.174 --> 00:01:58.021
Three more are bands of
motion on the held note.

00:01:58.421 --> 00:02:02.262
The bands run from half a hertz
to two, and from two to eight.

00:02:02.262 --> 00:02:04.616
The fastest runs from eight to thirty.

00:02:05.016 --> 00:02:08.876
Twenty-six structural features come
from the patch, with no render.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:14.430
Live, the same compiler builds
four voices inside an AudioWorklet.

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:18.577
Four more play PERFORM's offers,
crossfaded at equal power.

00:02:18.977 --> 00:02:22.115
In steady state, the audio
thread allocates nothing.

00:02:22.515 --> 00:02:27.493
A patch change fades out, rebuilds the
voices in silence, and carries your held

00:02:27.493 --> 00:02:28.493
notes across.

00:02:29.486 --> 00:02:32.806
Auditions render in parallel,
on up to six workers.

00:02:33.206 --> 00:02:38.113
Draws are indexed and absorbed in order,
so the pool is identical at any width.

00:02:39.486 --> 00:02:44.473
One compiler serves search and stage, so
what you play is what the model measured.
